WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.400
Welcome back to the Deep Dive. I want to start today by asking you to do something a little weird.

00:06.780 --> 00:10.900
Wherever you are, unless you're driving, please keep your hands on the wheel if you're driving.

00:11.300 --> 00:15.700
But if you can, I want you to look at your hand. Just hold it up in front of your face,

00:15.920 --> 00:18.500
wiggle your fingers, look at the thumb, really look at it.

00:18.540 --> 00:23.600
Okay, I'm looking at mine. It's a hand. Five fingers, palm, lines.

00:23.760 --> 00:26.980
It's a marvel of engineering, right? The dexterity, the grip strength.

00:26.980 --> 00:31.600
But it's also a biological machine with very real limits.

00:32.060 --> 00:36.840
It has muscles that get tired. It has nerves that have a maximum transmission speed.

00:36.960 --> 00:41.000
It has a specific range of motion. It cramps up if you use it too much in one position.

00:41.540 --> 00:48.020
And today, we're going to talk about how that biological machine, that fleshy, cramping, energy-burning tool,

00:48.320 --> 00:51.200
actually shaped the way we think and the way we communicate.

00:51.200 --> 00:57.520
We're talking about writing, but not in the way you usually hear about it in history class or literature class.

00:57.580 --> 00:59.300
We aren't talking about the content of the books.

00:59.480 --> 01:04.520
We are talking about the physical act of putting ink on paper.

01:04.780 --> 01:10.420
Right. Usually when we talk about the alphabet, it's this very abstract intellectual history.

01:10.840 --> 01:16.540
Like, the Phoenicians invented the letter A, and then the Greeks rotated it, and the Romans standardized it.

01:16.540 --> 01:22.920
It feels very clean, very academic. It feels like a decision made by minds floating in a void.

01:23.040 --> 01:30.460
As if it were designed by a committee of graphic designers sitting in a pristine white room with unlimited time and resources,

01:30.740 --> 01:33.360
trying to make the most logical system possible.

01:33.520 --> 01:37.900
Exactly. But the source material we have today argues the complete opposite.

01:38.720 --> 01:43.900
It argues that logic had very little to do with it, and biology had everything to do with it.

01:43.900 --> 01:51.700
We are diving into a fascinating paper titled Embodied Constraint and Minimal Assembly by Fliction,

01:52.180 --> 01:55.200
published just this year, February 14, 2026.

01:55.540 --> 02:00.000
And the core argument here is that writing is messy. It is a physical struggle.

02:00.180 --> 02:05.520
It's a struggle between your hand, which is fundamentally lazy, and your eye, which is fundamentally picky.

02:05.880 --> 02:08.380
I love that framing. Laziness versus pickiness.

02:08.380 --> 02:17.740
Yes. And the battlefield where this war took place, the case study for this deep dive, is the Arabic script.

02:18.880 --> 02:21.140
Now, I want to be clear to everyone listening.

02:22.100 --> 02:28.040
Even if you do not speak a word of Arabic, even if you've never tried to read it, you need to stick around.

02:28.460 --> 02:30.540
Because this isn't really a linguistics lesson.

02:30.540 --> 02:35.720
It's a story about human physiology, thermodynamics, and information theory colliding.

02:36.020 --> 02:37.920
It's about how we optimize energy.

02:38.620 --> 02:41.340
The paper uses this term embodied constraint.

02:41.900 --> 02:45.400
It sounds heavy, almost philosophical, but it's actually quite intuitive.

02:45.900 --> 02:47.760
Embodied just means in the body.

02:48.060 --> 02:51.520
Writing isn't magic. It's a motor act. It happens in the meat of your hand.

02:52.040 --> 02:53.760
And constraint means limits.

02:54.120 --> 02:57.220
You can only move your pen so fast before you lose control.

02:57.220 --> 03:00.040
You can only write so small before your muscles cramp.

03:00.420 --> 03:04.060
You can only remember so many complex shapes before your brain gets overloaded.

03:04.360 --> 03:10.860
And the paper pairs that with the second concept in the title, minimal assembly, which immediately made me think of furniture.

03:11.180 --> 03:14.060
It is the IKEA principle of language, in a way.

03:14.280 --> 03:18.260
Explain that. How is an ancient alphabet like a flat-packed bookshelf?

03:18.680 --> 03:20.380
Well, think about IKEA furniture.

03:21.100 --> 03:26.480
They don't give you a custom-carved Baroque chair that was sculpted from a single giant log.

03:26.480 --> 03:29.000
That would be expensive and hard to transport.

03:29.740 --> 03:32.480
Instead, they give you a few standardized parts.

03:33.000 --> 03:35.480
A screw, a dowel, a plank, a bracket.

03:36.040 --> 03:39.860
And by combining those few cheap standardized parts in different ways,

03:40.000 --> 03:43.420
you can build a bed, a desk, a chair, or a shelf.

03:44.080 --> 03:47.040
The paper argues that writing systems evolve the same way.

03:47.440 --> 03:51.980
You don't have a unique, complex painting for every single word in the language.

03:52.060 --> 03:53.680
That would be impossible to learn.

03:53.680 --> 03:55.540
Instead, you have a kit of parts.

03:55.920 --> 03:58.360
A curve, a line, a dot, a loop.

03:58.540 --> 03:59.180
That's it.

03:59.540 --> 04:04.020
And you have to assemble those cheap parts to capture the entire universe of human thought.

04:04.240 --> 04:10.020
And the driving force behind this assembly, and this is the part that really blew my mind, is thermodynamics.

04:10.680 --> 04:12.760
The author actually brings physics into this.

04:12.920 --> 04:13.240
Yes.

04:13.780 --> 04:15.980
This is where Flitchin gets really interesting.

04:16.140 --> 04:18.960
He calls writing a dissipative act.

04:18.960 --> 04:21.380
Which is a fancy way of saying it burns calories.

04:21.980 --> 04:23.100
It burns calories.

04:23.500 --> 04:23.840
Literally.

04:24.380 --> 04:29.060
Every stroke of the pen requires adenosine triphosphate ATP.

04:29.420 --> 04:31.280
It requires metabolic energy.

04:31.720 --> 04:35.100
Your heart has to pump blood to the muscles in your forearm.

04:35.240 --> 04:36.640
Your neurons have to fire.

04:37.380 --> 04:42.460
And the human body is wired through millions of years of evolution to conserve energy.

04:43.080 --> 04:45.100
We are biological misers.

04:45.460 --> 04:48.100
We hate spending calories if we don't have to.

04:48.100 --> 04:51.700
Because for most of human history, calories were scarce.

04:52.060 --> 04:55.060
So if you can do something with less effort, you will.

04:55.200 --> 04:55.720
Exactly.

04:56.060 --> 05:00.720
So if you leave a writer alone for a thousand years, their handwriting will naturally degrade.

05:01.120 --> 05:03.760
It will get simpler, faster, and messier.

05:04.240 --> 05:05.540
Curves will straighten out.

05:05.820 --> 05:07.680
Distinct shapes will merge together.

05:08.040 --> 05:09.500
That's the thermodynamic pressure.

05:09.640 --> 05:10.400
That's the entropy.

05:10.680 --> 05:11.640
That's the laziness.

05:11.940 --> 05:12.720
But here's the catch.

05:12.720 --> 05:19.720
If you let that laziness win completely, if you go for 100% efficiency, your writing just becomes a straight line.

05:19.960 --> 05:20.740
It becomes a hump.

05:20.780 --> 05:23.580
And a straight line contains zero information.

05:23.740 --> 05:24.040
Zero.

05:24.440 --> 05:31.340
If I hand you a paper with a single horizontal line on it, you have no idea if I'm saying, hello, I love you, or war is declared.

05:31.340 --> 05:32.960
It's completely ambiguous.

05:33.560 --> 05:39.220
So you have this opposing force, the eye, the reader, or really the decoder.

05:39.560 --> 05:41.420
The reader demands distinctness.

05:42.400 --> 05:45.500
The reader needs the shapes to be different enough to tell them apart.

05:45.500 --> 05:53.500
So the script we see today, specifically the Arabic script, is describing the paper as a stabilized equilibrium.

05:54.340 --> 05:55.180
It's a truce line.

05:55.280 --> 06:02.280
It's the exact point where the hand does the minimum amount of work possible without the eye losing the ability to read it.

06:02.520 --> 06:04.080
It's an economic negotiation.

06:04.700 --> 06:08.300
I will spend this many calories to make this curve, but not a single calorie more.

06:08.900 --> 06:11.020
If I spend less, you can't read it.

06:11.100 --> 06:12.980
If I spend more, I'm wasting energy.

06:12.980 --> 06:19.840
So the alphabet basically evolved to save calories while keeping us from misreading a text.

06:20.080 --> 06:21.760
That is essentially the thesis, yes.

06:21.840 --> 06:25.500
And what's amazing is looking at how that negotiation played out over history.

06:25.800 --> 06:27.900
Because it wasn't a straight line of progress.

06:28.220 --> 06:30.680
It was a messy, tiered process.

06:31.080 --> 06:35.200
It involved mistakes, patches, and updates, just like software.

06:35.500 --> 06:36.620
Let's get into that history.

06:37.100 --> 06:38.740
The source breaks this down into segments.

06:39.100 --> 06:41.980
And the first one is about the skeleton of the script, the rasm.

06:41.980 --> 06:43.060
The rasm.

06:43.400 --> 06:45.400
This is the primitive layer of the script.

06:46.120 --> 06:55.180
If you go back to the 7th century, to the very earliest manuscripts of the Quran, or the early administrative papyri, what you see is stunning.

06:55.360 --> 06:56.600
It's visually beautiful.

06:56.860 --> 07:00.880
It's these sweeping, long, curvy lines.

07:01.140 --> 07:03.240
It looks like waves on an ocean.

07:03.340 --> 07:04.840
But there's a massive problem with it.

07:04.840 --> 07:05.940
A massive problem.

07:06.320 --> 07:09.240
It's almost unreadable by modern standards.

07:09.360 --> 07:12.560
In fact, even experts struggle with it sometimes without context.

07:12.920 --> 07:13.000
Why?

07:13.160 --> 07:15.480
If it's the same language, why is it unreadable?

07:15.640 --> 07:18.160
Because of that motor efficiency we just talked about.

07:18.580 --> 07:20.080
The collabs were writing fast.

07:20.180 --> 07:20.900
They were flowing.

07:21.240 --> 07:26.300
And because they were flowing, they allowed the shapes of different letters to collapse into each other.

07:26.300 --> 07:30.500
They optimized the distinctness right out of the letters.

07:30.680 --> 07:34.300
The source mentioned specifically the letters for B, T, and TH.

07:34.980 --> 07:37.920
In Arabic, that's Ba, Ta, and Tai.

07:38.300 --> 07:38.620
Right.

07:39.060 --> 07:40.900
So let's think about the sounds first.

07:41.840 --> 07:44.200
B is a voiced sound using the lips.

07:44.600 --> 07:46.620
T is a sharp stop behind the teeth.

07:47.000 --> 07:48.080
The is a fricative.

07:48.080 --> 07:50.780
They sound totally different.

07:50.880 --> 07:54.420
If I say bat, tat, and that, those are clearly different words.

07:54.940 --> 08:00.040
But in the rasm, the skeletal script, the symbols for these sounds are identical.

08:00.280 --> 08:03.100
They're just a little tooth, a little bump on the line.

08:03.300 --> 08:03.440
Wait.

08:03.500 --> 08:08.960
So if I'm reading a text from the year 650 and I see a bump, just a little ripple in the line.

08:09.040 --> 08:11.020
It could be a B, a T, or a TH.

08:11.460 --> 08:11.860
Yes.

08:12.300 --> 08:13.280
And it gets worse.

08:13.280 --> 08:20.300
That same bump shape is also used for N, none, and Y, yeah, in the beginning or middle of a word.

08:20.480 --> 08:25.520
So you have five different letters sharing the exact same chassis, the exact same skeleton.

08:25.760 --> 08:26.600
That sounds like a nightmare.

08:27.420 --> 08:31.580
Imagine reading English where B, B, key, Q, and G were all just written as a circle,

08:31.820 --> 08:37.980
and you had to figure out if the word was bad, dad, pad, or gag just by guessing.

08:38.320 --> 08:39.560
That is a perfect analogy.

08:39.680 --> 08:41.340
You're relying entirely on context.

08:41.340 --> 08:44.960
The man sat on the, well, it's probably pad or bed, not dad.

08:45.940 --> 08:47.360
You act as a detective.

08:47.660 --> 08:48.820
You aren't just reading.

08:48.980 --> 08:49.980
You are deciphering.

08:50.120 --> 08:52.720
But that requires a lot of cognitive energy.

08:52.980 --> 08:56.680
We were talking about saving physical energy, but now we're burning brain power.

08:57.140 --> 08:58.460
Huge cognitive load.

08:58.840 --> 09:06.120
And it works fine if you already know the text by heart, which, to be fair, many early readers of the Quran did.

09:06.680 --> 09:08.300
It was an oral tradition first.

09:08.300 --> 09:11.700
The writing was just a mnemonic device, a memory aid.

09:11.900 --> 09:17.480
Ah, so the text was just a reminder of what you already knew, like sheet music for a song you've already memorized.

09:17.800 --> 09:18.000
Yeah.

09:18.020 --> 09:19.740
You just need to see the shape of the melody.

09:20.100 --> 09:20.540
Exactly.

09:21.040 --> 09:22.340
But then history happens.

09:22.480 --> 09:23.440
The empire grows.

09:23.440 --> 09:30.500
You have non-native speakers in Persia, in Egypt, in Spain, converting to Islam and trying to learn Arabic.

09:31.240 --> 09:32.740
They don't have the text memorized.

09:33.160 --> 09:36.760
They don't have that native intuition to guess the context.

09:37.020 --> 09:39.560
They look at these bumps and they are lost.

09:40.000 --> 09:41.780
The ambiguity becomes too expensive.

09:42.200 --> 09:43.780
The system is failing.

09:43.780 --> 09:46.000
So the pressure for clarity spikes.

09:46.820 --> 09:48.240
The laziness has gone too far.

09:48.480 --> 09:49.840
The equilibrium is broken.

09:50.240 --> 09:51.220
How do they fix it?

09:51.600 --> 09:55.860
This is where the concept of minimal assembly really shines.

09:56.440 --> 10:01.020
If this were a modern software project, you might say, okay, this code is broken.

10:01.240 --> 10:03.480
Let's delete it and rewrite the whole thing.

10:03.720 --> 10:08.380
Let's invent 28 totally new shapes so B looks nothing like T.

10:08.840 --> 10:09.860
But they didn't do that.

10:09.960 --> 10:11.020
That would be a hard fork.

10:11.020 --> 10:13.440
No, that's too expensive.

10:13.980 --> 10:17.540
That requires retraining an entire civilization.

10:18.260 --> 10:20.980
It requires throwing away all the books you've already written.

10:21.120 --> 10:22.580
Instead, they patched it.

10:22.660 --> 10:25.120
They introduced incremental augmentation.

10:25.500 --> 10:25.780
The dot.

10:25.940 --> 10:26.380
The dot.

10:26.740 --> 10:27.480
The humble dot.

10:27.980 --> 10:32.820
It is perhaps the most important technological invention in the history of the Arabic script.

10:33.220 --> 10:36.400
I really loved the economics of the dot section in the paper.

10:36.800 --> 10:39.740
It treats the dot like a piece of high-tech engineering.

10:39.740 --> 10:41.080
It is a piece of technology.

10:41.240 --> 10:43.020
Think about the motor cost of a dot.

10:43.280 --> 10:46.480
It is the smallest possible mark you can make with a pen.

10:46.600 --> 10:47.440
It's just a tap.

10:47.640 --> 10:50.100
Pen down, pen up, zero drag.

10:50.860 --> 10:51.940
Minimal ink usage.

10:52.620 --> 10:54.440
It's cheap, energy-wise.

10:54.700 --> 10:57.920
You aren't drawing a complex drag and you're just tapping the page.

10:58.020 --> 11:00.520
But the informational return is massive.

11:01.040 --> 11:01.760
Infinite return.

11:01.760 --> 11:07.220
By adding one cheap dot below that ambiguous bump, it becomes a B.

11:08.040 --> 11:09.660
Two dots above, it's a T.

11:10.320 --> 11:11.780
Three dots, it's a T.

11:12.460 --> 11:13.800
One dot above, it's an N.

11:13.940 --> 11:15.560
Two dots below, it's a Y.

11:16.500 --> 11:21.660
You have resolved the ambiguity of five different letters without breaking the flow of the script.

11:21.660 --> 11:25.740
So you keep the lazy flowing skeleton because it's fast to write.

11:25.880 --> 11:26.580
That's layer one.

11:27.080 --> 11:33.420
And then you come back and add these tiny high-fidelity data packets, the dots to specify the meaning.

11:33.740 --> 11:34.860
That's layer two.

11:35.200 --> 11:36.460
It's a dual-layer system.

11:36.580 --> 11:37.600
It's incredibly sophisticated.

11:37.940 --> 11:40.300
It's like a compression algorithm in your computer.

11:40.680 --> 11:49.260
You transmit the low-res version first, the skeleton, and then you sharpen the image with the high-res details, the dots, only where you need them.

11:49.260 --> 11:51.820
It's amazing that this wasn't designed by a committee.

11:52.020 --> 11:52.960
It just happened.

11:53.080 --> 11:53.740
It evolved.

11:53.960 --> 11:56.040
It's survival of the fittest futures.

11:56.280 --> 12:00.600
Now, this is where the paper takes a turn that I found absolutely fascinating.

12:01.220 --> 12:06.420
We know that they use dots, but the source talks about where the dots go and how many dots.

12:06.800 --> 12:08.200
Because it turns out it's not random.

12:08.640 --> 12:13.200
There's a deep, hidden connection between the dots on the page and the physics of your mouth.

12:13.520 --> 12:13.880
Yes.

12:14.340 --> 12:16.740
This is the phonological markedness argument.

12:16.740 --> 12:20.200
This is where linguistics and graphic design merge.

12:20.640 --> 12:21.560
Break that down for us.

12:21.980 --> 12:22.460
Markedness.

12:22.600 --> 12:23.220
What does that mean?

12:23.560 --> 12:24.780
It's a linguistics term.

12:25.280 --> 12:29.220
In any language, there are sounds that are easy or default.

12:29.600 --> 12:30.840
We call those unmarked.

12:31.440 --> 12:33.760
They are the path of least resistance for the human mouth.

12:34.220 --> 12:36.120
They are the sounds a baby makes first.

12:36.260 --> 12:37.200
Da-da-da-da.

12:37.400 --> 12:37.920
Exactly.

12:38.220 --> 12:39.960
And then there are sounds that are marked.

12:40.320 --> 12:41.500
They require something extra.

12:41.600 --> 12:42.740
They require extra effort.

12:42.740 --> 12:50.000
You have to buzz your vocal cords or blow more air or twist your tongue in a weird way or hold the sound longer.

12:50.460 --> 12:56.660
So unmarked is the default setting and marked is the upgrade package that costs extra energy.

12:56.800 --> 12:57.100
Correct.

12:58.100 --> 13:04.880
And the Fliction paper argues that the Arabic script unconsciously mapped this physical reality onto the paper.

13:05.560 --> 13:08.940
Extra effort in the mouth equals extra ink on the page.

13:08.940 --> 13:12.140
Let's look at the examples because this is where you can really see it.

13:12.520 --> 13:16.700
The paper brings up D versus D-H, del and dal.

13:16.900 --> 13:17.220
Right.

13:17.560 --> 13:19.640
So I want everyone listening to try this.

13:19.880 --> 13:23.300
Say the letter D, da, door, day.

13:23.780 --> 13:24.780
Where is your tongue?

13:25.040 --> 13:27.380
It's just tapping the roots of my mouth behind my teeth.

13:27.660 --> 13:28.060
Duh.

13:28.440 --> 13:29.140
It's a stop.

13:29.380 --> 13:31.060
You block the air, then you release it.

13:31.120 --> 13:32.660
It's very simple ballistics.

13:32.660 --> 13:36.080
In the script, the letter del has no dot.

13:36.520 --> 13:37.480
It is just a curve.

13:37.780 --> 13:39.780
It is the unmarked basic shape.

13:39.920 --> 13:40.160
Okay.

13:40.560 --> 13:41.840
Simple sound, simple shape.

13:42.220 --> 13:43.160
Now say D-H.

13:43.900 --> 13:46.960
This is the sound in the words the or this or that.

13:47.300 --> 13:48.580
In Arabic, it's del.

13:49.540 --> 13:50.020
The.

13:50.960 --> 13:51.420
This.

13:51.820 --> 13:53.020
What is your tongue doing now?

13:53.140 --> 13:54.380
It's sliding forward.

13:54.540 --> 13:55.820
It's actually between my teeth.

13:56.240 --> 13:58.220
And I'm blowing air through it.

13:58.240 --> 13:59.820
You are creating turbulence.

14:00.240 --> 14:01.760
That is an interdental fricative.

14:01.760 --> 14:05.020
You are engaging the tongue and the breath and the teeth.

14:05.200 --> 14:06.740
And you have to sustain it.

14:06.860 --> 14:10.020
It is physically more complex than a simple D.

14:10.520 --> 14:11.620
It is marked.

14:11.800 --> 14:12.500
And in the script.

14:13.000 --> 14:13.760
It gets a dot.

14:14.060 --> 14:16.640
The del shape plus a dot equals del.

14:17.100 --> 14:21.720
The extra physical friction of the tongue is represented by the extra mark of the pen.

14:21.980 --> 14:22.800
That is so cool.

14:22.880 --> 14:25.440
It's like the script is a diagram of the effort.

14:25.840 --> 14:28.520
Here is the base move and here is the extra friction.

14:28.620 --> 14:29.060
Exactly.

14:29.200 --> 14:29.920
Give us another one.

14:29.920 --> 14:31.460
Okay, let's take R versus Z.

14:31.740 --> 14:32.440
Ra and Zay.

14:32.660 --> 14:34.420
They look exactly the same in the skeleton.

14:34.960 --> 14:36.000
But Zay has a dot.

14:36.280 --> 14:40.520
So the R in Arabic is a tap or a trill.

14:41.180 --> 14:41.440
Ra.

14:41.900 --> 14:42.700
It's quick.

14:42.920 --> 14:45.080
The tongue just flaps against the roof of the mouth.

14:45.320 --> 14:45.860
No dot.

14:46.320 --> 14:47.020
But the Z.

14:47.560 --> 14:47.880
Z.

14:48.140 --> 14:49.860
I can feel my throat buzzing.

14:49.860 --> 14:51.540
That's your vocal cords vibrating.

14:51.740 --> 14:52.660
That's called voicing.

14:53.080 --> 14:56.560
You have to sustain the air pressure and engage the larynx.

14:56.640 --> 15:00.040
It costs more energy to say zizz than rawr.

15:00.380 --> 15:01.400
So what happens?

15:01.500 --> 15:02.700
The Z gets a dot.

15:02.780 --> 15:04.520
Extra ink equals extra sound.

15:04.600 --> 15:05.560
It's so consistent.

15:05.820 --> 15:09.140
And the ultimate example is the S versus the shish.

15:09.320 --> 15:10.540
The sin and the shin.

15:10.540 --> 15:12.560
Oh, the shin has the most dots, right?

15:12.820 --> 15:13.740
It has three dots.

15:13.900 --> 15:14.900
The maximum payload.

15:15.100 --> 15:16.420
So let's trace the physics.

15:16.860 --> 15:18.920
The S sounds in is a hiss.

15:19.320 --> 15:19.600
Th.

15:19.860 --> 15:20.780
It's a strident.

15:21.580 --> 15:24.080
It's a sharp, clear stream of air.

15:24.500 --> 15:27.000
The letter shape looks like three little teeth.

15:27.360 --> 15:27.980
No dots.

15:28.060 --> 15:29.160
It's the standard hiss.

15:29.160 --> 15:32.420
And the sh-shin, like in shu or shout.

15:32.680 --> 15:32.920
Shh.

15:33.320 --> 15:33.640
Do it.

15:33.740 --> 15:34.280
Feel your mouth.

15:34.520 --> 15:35.600
You have to pull your tongue back.

15:35.860 --> 15:40.020
You have to dome the middle of your tongue up towards the hard palate that's called postal

15:40.020 --> 15:40.640
violer.

15:40.700 --> 15:41.020
Mm-hmm.

15:41.140 --> 15:43.760
And you had to blow a huge volume of turbulent air.

15:43.880 --> 15:46.740
It is a chaotic, high-energy sound.

15:46.840 --> 15:48.140
It washes over everything.

15:48.560 --> 15:51.240
And the script responds by dumping three dots on it.

15:51.640 --> 15:52.780
It's like a warning label.

15:53.340 --> 15:53.780
Caution.

15:54.860 --> 15:56.280
Heavy turbulence ahead.

15:56.280 --> 16:01.160
It's the most complex sound in that group, so it gets the most complex marking.

16:01.620 --> 16:03.020
Do you think the scribes knew this?

16:03.320 --> 16:08.300
Do they sit down and say, well, the postal violer fricative is complex, so let's add three

16:08.300 --> 16:08.660
dots?

16:09.060 --> 16:10.320
Almost certainly not.

16:10.400 --> 16:11.800
They didn't have MRI machines.

16:12.020 --> 16:14.700
They didn't have the vocabulary of modern linguistics.

16:14.940 --> 16:16.540
But they had their bodies.

16:16.720 --> 16:17.540
They felt the difference.

16:17.800 --> 16:24.040
They felt that she was heavier than S. They felt that Z was buzzier than R. And that somatic

16:24.040 --> 16:27.580
bodily intuition guided the evolution of the script.

16:27.900 --> 16:31.920
They added weight to the letter because they felt weight in the mouth.

16:32.280 --> 16:34.680
It really drives home the main point of the deep dive.

16:35.340 --> 16:37.400
The script is a recording of the body.

16:37.580 --> 16:39.100
It's not arbitrary squiggles.

16:39.380 --> 16:40.560
It's anatomical.

16:40.700 --> 16:43.100
It is the body leaving a trail in the sand.

16:43.100 --> 16:46.980
Speaking of anatomy, this leads us perfectly into segment two.

16:47.520 --> 16:51.840
Because if markedness is about the throat and the vocal cords, this next part is about

16:51.840 --> 16:52.700
the tongue itself.

16:53.320 --> 16:55.540
We need to talk about the sun and moon letters.

16:55.740 --> 16:56.560
Ah, yes.

16:56.960 --> 17:00.440
The bane of every Arabic student's existence.

17:00.860 --> 17:01.840
I remember trying to learn this.

17:02.240 --> 17:03.640
The teacher just hands you a list.

17:04.440 --> 17:05.400
Here are 14 letters.

17:06.340 --> 17:08.800
If the word starts with these, you pronounce the L.

17:09.440 --> 17:11.140
If it starts with those, you don't.

17:11.140 --> 17:11.840
Good luck.

17:12.040 --> 17:12.820
Memorize it.

17:12.920 --> 17:13.760
Don't ask why.

17:14.060 --> 17:14.140
Right.

17:14.260 --> 17:19.140
So for the listeners who don't know, in Arabic, the word for the is al.

17:19.840 --> 17:22.900
Like Al Jazeera or Al Qaeda or Al Qaeda.

17:23.400 --> 17:24.380
You hear the L.

17:24.820 --> 17:25.220
Clearly.

17:25.620 --> 17:25.920
Al.

17:25.980 --> 17:26.940
Those are the moon letters.

17:27.220 --> 17:28.460
The L stays strong.

17:28.600 --> 17:29.160
It behaves.

17:29.640 --> 17:31.360
But then you have words like the sun.

17:31.960 --> 17:33.640
In Arabic, sun is shams.

17:33.840 --> 17:34.820
You don't say al shams.

17:34.900 --> 17:36.000
You say ash shams.

17:36.180 --> 17:37.040
The L disappears.

17:37.520 --> 17:38.000
It melts.

17:38.240 --> 17:39.360
And the she gets doubled.

17:39.360 --> 17:40.360
Or the people.

17:41.140 --> 17:41.740
Anos.

17:42.420 --> 17:43.320
Not elnas.

17:43.520 --> 17:44.740
The L becomes an N.

17:45.000 --> 17:45.200
Why?

17:45.420 --> 17:50.420
Why does the L surrender to the S or the N or the T, but it stands its ground against

17:50.420 --> 17:51.300
the K or the B?

17:51.780 --> 17:56.300
If you ask a traditional grammarian, they might give you a poetic answer about the sun burning

17:56.300 --> 17:57.040
up the stars.

17:57.420 --> 17:59.620
But Flixian says, look at the mouth.

17:59.740 --> 18:00.480
It's not poetry.

18:00.660 --> 18:01.760
It's biomechanics.

18:01.860 --> 18:02.820
It's the ton map.

18:03.080 --> 18:03.460
Exactly.

18:03.640 --> 18:04.320
Let's do the anatomy.

18:04.500 --> 18:05.540
Where is the sound L made?

18:05.960 --> 18:06.640
Say so.

18:06.640 --> 18:11.080
My tongue is touching the roof of my mouth right behind my front teeth.

18:11.260 --> 18:11.620
Correct.

18:11.980 --> 18:13.520
That is the coronal region.

18:13.920 --> 18:14.800
The crown of the tongue.

18:14.880 --> 18:16.020
Now let's look at the sun letters.

18:16.180 --> 18:17.560
The ones that make the L melt.

18:18.100 --> 18:21.100
T, D, S, Z, N, R, S, H.

18:21.740 --> 18:22.560
Go through them.

18:22.960 --> 18:23.680
Where is your tongue?

18:24.240 --> 18:24.440
T.

18:24.660 --> 18:25.640
Right behind the teeth.

18:26.820 --> 18:27.100
D.

18:27.200 --> 18:27.760
Same spot.

18:28.060 --> 18:28.840
S, same spot.

18:29.220 --> 18:30.280
N, same stuff.

18:30.580 --> 18:32.140
They are all coronal sounds.

18:32.280 --> 18:33.000
They are all neighbors.

18:33.260 --> 18:36.580
They all live in the same tiny apartment complex right behind your teeth.

18:36.820 --> 18:39.000
So what's happening when I try to say L shums?

18:39.320 --> 18:40.460
Think about the gymnastics.

18:40.920 --> 18:43.140
Your tongue goes up to the teeth for L.

18:43.820 --> 18:46.400
Then it has to pull back a millimeter for shh.

18:46.900 --> 18:48.180
It's a stutter step.

18:48.720 --> 18:51.340
It's a rapid fire double tap in the same zone.

18:51.440 --> 18:52.060
It's inefficient.

18:52.700 --> 18:53.700
The tongue is lazy.

18:53.900 --> 18:55.620
It says, look, I'm already right here.

18:55.700 --> 18:58.460
Why don't I just skip the L and lean harder into the shit?

18:58.660 --> 18:59.280
It's a shortcut.

18:59.940 --> 19:01.040
It is assimilation.

19:01.040 --> 19:05.520
The tongue merges the two movements into one fluid gesture.

19:06.060 --> 19:06.540
Ashams.

19:06.880 --> 19:07.440
It flows.

19:07.660 --> 19:08.520
It saves energy.

19:08.700 --> 19:11.000
With a moon letter like K or B.

19:11.140 --> 19:11.600
Say K.

19:11.880 --> 19:12.400
Alkitab.

19:12.760 --> 19:14.480
K is made way back in the throat.

19:14.680 --> 19:15.140
The vellum.

19:15.440 --> 19:16.680
It is far away from the L.

19:17.080 --> 19:18.480
So the tongue has to move anyway.

19:18.980 --> 19:19.460
Exactly.

19:19.720 --> 19:23.100
You have to travel from the teeth, L, to the throat, K.

19:23.300 --> 19:24.620
There is no shortcut available.

19:25.200 --> 19:27.460
Since you have to make the trip across the mouth anyway,

19:27.780 --> 19:29.540
you might as well pronounce the L.

19:29.540 --> 19:31.180
Oh, that makes so much sense.

19:31.300 --> 19:35.120
So the moon letters are just sounds that are geographically distant from the L.

19:35.820 --> 19:38.360
And sun letters are the neighbors.

19:38.620 --> 19:39.940
It's not a grammar rule.

19:40.060 --> 19:42.380
It's a tongue cramp prevention strategy.

19:42.760 --> 19:46.360
It's your mouth naturally optimizing the path of motion.

19:46.480 --> 19:47.220
I love that.

19:47.340 --> 19:51.340
And the source points out a visual coincidence here that I find really funny.

19:51.820 --> 19:52.620
It's a heuristic.

19:52.940 --> 19:53.300
Yes.

19:53.660 --> 19:54.740
The toothy theory.

19:55.160 --> 19:57.760
Many of these sun letters, these tip of the tongue sounds,

19:57.760 --> 20:00.500
actually look like teeth in the script.

20:00.840 --> 20:01.820
Ideally, yes.

20:02.180 --> 20:03.520
The sin has three teeth.

20:04.000 --> 20:05.860
The sad has a loop in a tooth.

20:06.180 --> 20:07.420
The shin has teeth.

20:07.880 --> 20:13.220
Even the ta and ta are basically wide, shallow dishes with dots that look like eyes or teeth.

20:13.440 --> 20:15.940
Now, the paper is careful to say this wasn't designed.

20:16.540 --> 20:17.700
The scribes didn't say,

20:18.200 --> 20:20.560
let's draw teeth because it's a tooth sound.

20:20.560 --> 20:22.500
No, that would be teleological.

20:23.240 --> 20:24.320
It would imply a plan.

20:24.740 --> 20:27.380
But it is a happy accident of evolution.

20:28.380 --> 20:32.920
Visually, the script inadvertently grouped these sounds into similar looking shapes.

20:33.120 --> 20:35.200
It matches the coronal clustering.

20:35.700 --> 20:39.060
It reminds me of the IPA chart, the International Phonetic Alphabet,

20:39.460 --> 20:42.060
where they map vowels based on where your tongue is.

20:42.060 --> 20:42.540
Exactly.

20:43.020 --> 20:46.180
Modern linguistics maps sounds by tongue position.

20:46.800 --> 20:48.940
Arabic orthography, the way it's written,

20:49.460 --> 20:53.740
intuitively group these consonants by articulatory proximity.

20:54.200 --> 20:56.340
It's like the script knows your mouth better than you do.

20:56.660 --> 20:59.360
I will never look at a sun letter the same way again.

20:59.580 --> 21:00.900
It's just my tongue being lazy.

21:01.100 --> 21:01.500
Efficient.

21:01.800 --> 21:02.140
Efficient.

21:02.380 --> 21:02.580
Okay.

21:02.960 --> 21:04.240
Moving on to segment three.

21:04.820 --> 21:07.480
We've talked about the skeleton, the dots, and the tongue.

21:07.480 --> 21:13.080
Now let's talk about the movement, the flow, the physics of cursive.

21:13.680 --> 21:15.740
This brings us back to that energy equation.

21:16.220 --> 21:17.980
Why do we write cursively at all?

21:18.120 --> 21:18.960
Why join letters?

21:19.160 --> 21:21.320
We take it for granted, but we don't have to.

21:21.560 --> 21:24.360
We could write like a typewriter, one letter at a time.

21:24.520 --> 21:24.900
Speed.

21:25.160 --> 21:26.180
Speed is the result.

21:26.440 --> 21:29.340
But the mechanism is reducing pen lifts.

21:29.480 --> 21:29.920
Pen lifts.

21:30.060 --> 21:31.060
Think about it physically.

21:31.540 --> 21:35.600
Every time you lift the pen off the paper, you are decelerating to zero.

21:35.600 --> 21:36.840
You have to stop.

21:36.960 --> 21:37.740
You have to hover.

21:38.120 --> 21:39.980
You have to re-aim to the next spot.

21:40.300 --> 21:42.380
You have to accelerate again to touch the paper.

21:42.600 --> 21:44.900
That is a massive waste of momentum.

21:45.360 --> 21:48.340
It's like driving in stop-and-go traffic versus driving on the highway.

21:48.620 --> 21:49.060
Exactly.

21:49.580 --> 21:50.520
Cursive is the highway.

21:51.000 --> 21:52.100
You keep the pen down.

21:52.280 --> 21:53.440
You preserve momentum.

21:53.960 --> 21:55.600
The ink flows continuously.

21:56.560 --> 22:01.160
Mathematically, more joins equals fewer lifts equals lower energy costs.

22:01.160 --> 22:07.540
So pure laziness, I'm sorry, efficiency, would dictate that we join absolutely everything.

22:07.720 --> 22:08.980
We should never lift the pen.

22:09.380 --> 22:12.280
One continuous line from the start of the book to the end.

22:12.680 --> 22:15.880
That would be the thermodynamic ideal, but there's a trap.

22:16.260 --> 22:17.000
The overshoot.

22:17.260 --> 22:18.640
What happens if you join everything?

22:18.900 --> 22:21.280
The letters lose their structural integrity.

22:21.840 --> 22:27.880
If you connect every loop to every line at high speed, everything starts to look like a sine wave.

22:28.240 --> 22:28.820
Just a wiggle.

22:28.820 --> 22:32.440
We've all seen someone's handwriting that looks like a seismograph reading.

22:32.840 --> 22:34.340
My doctor's prescription pad.

22:34.440 --> 22:37.820
I have no idea if it says ibuprofen or amputation.

22:38.340 --> 22:38.940
Exactly.

22:39.640 --> 22:43.240
You have maximized flow, but you have destroyed clarity.

22:43.580 --> 22:46.640
You have minimized energy, but you've maximized confusion.

22:47.000 --> 22:48.700
So how does Arabic handle this?

22:49.040 --> 22:51.440
Because Arabic is extremely cursive.

22:51.840 --> 22:53.480
It flows more than English cursive.

22:53.980 --> 22:56.680
It is, but it has a built-in braking system.

22:56.940 --> 22:57.800
And this is genius.

22:57.800 --> 23:03.480
There are six specific letters in the Arabic alphabet that refuse to connect to the left.

23:03.840 --> 23:04.940
The non-connecting letters.

23:05.200 --> 23:10.020
They connect to the right, so you can flow into them from the previous letter, but you cannot flow out of them.

23:10.400 --> 23:14.200
You hit the letter, and the script forces you to lift the pen.

23:14.200 --> 23:23.720
These are the letters A, Elif, D, Dal, DH, Dal, R, R, Z, Z, and W, Y.

23:24.000 --> 23:24.360
Correct.

23:25.020 --> 23:26.540
Now, why these six?

23:26.680 --> 23:27.460
Is it random?

23:27.800 --> 23:29.620
The expert in the paper says no.

23:29.920 --> 23:32.140
They act as structural boundary markers.

23:32.140 --> 23:35.020
The source calls them controlled interruptions.

23:35.140 --> 23:37.300
I love that term, controlled interruptions.

23:37.460 --> 23:38.320
Look at the Aleph.

23:38.440 --> 23:39.700
It's a vertical line.

23:39.820 --> 23:41.180
Just a stick, up and down.

23:41.520 --> 23:49.420
If you let that stick connect to the left, and you're writing quickly, it would look exactly like the letter Lamb, L, which is also a vertical line that connects.

23:49.420 --> 23:53.340
So, if Aleph connected, you wouldn't know if you're reading an A or an L.

23:53.540 --> 23:53.780
Right.

23:53.980 --> 23:58.820
And since A and L are two of the most common letters, that would be a disaster.

23:59.580 --> 24:04.340
By forcing a pen lift after the Aleph, the script creates a patch of white space.

24:05.160 --> 24:07.140
That white space signals to the eye.

24:07.780 --> 24:09.820
This vertical line stands alone.

24:10.180 --> 24:10.980
This is an A.

24:11.380 --> 24:13.200
Now, what about the D, the Dal?

24:13.500 --> 24:14.280
Same issue.

24:14.520 --> 24:16.720
The Dal is a small, curved shape.

24:16.720 --> 24:23.360
If it connected to the left, it would look like a little tooth in the middle of a word, exactly like a B, T, N, or Y.

24:23.560 --> 24:25.260
It would disappear into the wave.

24:25.960 --> 24:29.860
By forcing a break, the script preserves the silhouette of the D.

24:30.280 --> 24:32.040
It says, this curve is important.

24:32.380 --> 24:33.140
Stop here.

24:33.780 --> 24:40.280
So, these non-connecting letters are basically the script saying, whoa, slow down, lift the pen, reset the eye.

24:40.400 --> 24:41.740
It's a negotiated peace treaty.

24:42.160 --> 24:43.440
The hand wants to keep moving.

24:43.540 --> 24:44.740
It wants to connect everything.

24:44.740 --> 24:47.520
The eye says, if you connect that, I can't read it.

24:47.940 --> 24:55.960
So, the compromise is, connect most things, but break the circuit at these six specific points to maintain structural integrity.

24:56.400 --> 25:02.620
It's fascinating to think of white space, the empty space between letters, as a functional part of the system.

25:03.200 --> 25:05.860
Usually, we think of the ink as the information.

25:06.600 --> 25:09.140
But the lack of ink is also information.

25:09.960 --> 25:11.600
Silence is part of the music.

25:12.180 --> 25:13.640
White space is part of the code.

25:13.640 --> 25:18.920
The paper makes a really interesting comparison here between Arabic and Hebrew.

25:19.660 --> 25:20.880
Because they're cousins, right?

25:20.980 --> 25:21.880
Very close cousins.

25:22.240 --> 25:26.080
They both come from the same Semitic ancestor, the Phoenician-Aramaic tradition.

25:26.380 --> 25:28.520
They share the same roots, the same logic.

25:29.060 --> 25:32.900
But they chose completely different strategies for this stability.

25:33.220 --> 25:33.660
Ah, so.

25:33.940 --> 25:36.640
Hebrew chose the strategy of discreteness.

25:36.640 --> 25:43.020
If you look at the Hebrew script, the square script used in Torah scrolls, the letters are separate blocks.

25:43.240 --> 25:44.480
They generally don't touch.

25:44.800 --> 25:46.920
It looks very architectural, like bricks and a wall.

25:47.140 --> 25:47.740
It is.

25:48.080 --> 25:49.780
This maximizes clarity.

25:50.120 --> 25:54.380
It is very hard to confuse Hebrew letters because they are so distinct and separated.

25:54.860 --> 25:56.700
But the cost is high energy.

25:56.700 --> 25:58.620
You are lifting the pen constantly.

25:58.960 --> 26:00.220
It's a high-friction script.

26:00.420 --> 26:01.660
It is slow to write.

26:02.280 --> 26:03.360
And Arabic went the other way.

26:03.720 --> 26:05.040
Arabic chose continuity.

26:05.580 --> 26:06.720
It maximized the flow.

26:06.860 --> 26:07.120
It said,

26:07.320 --> 26:17.100
We will accept the risk of ambiguity letters looking alike, and we will fix it with dots and context in exchange for the speed and low energy of cursive writing.

26:17.100 --> 26:19.700
That is a fascinating divergence.

26:20.220 --> 26:22.160
One went for stability through separation.

26:22.820 --> 26:24.760
The other for stability through flow.

26:25.060 --> 26:27.060
And you can even see it in the culture of the scripts.

26:27.720 --> 26:31.740
Hebrew feels monumental, timeless, static.

26:32.320 --> 26:34.080
It's carved in stone.

26:35.040 --> 26:37.520
Arabic feels kinetic, gestural.

26:37.720 --> 26:38.680
It's a fluid line.

26:38.800 --> 26:39.940
It's wind or water.

26:40.220 --> 26:43.320
It's like the difference between building a stone wall and flowing water.

26:43.620 --> 26:46.460
Both are strong, but in very different ways.

26:46.780 --> 26:47.580
Beautifully put.

26:47.760 --> 26:49.720
And that fluidity isn't just in the script.

26:49.860 --> 26:51.120
It's in the language itself.

26:51.400 --> 26:53.560
Which brings us to the engine under the hood.

26:53.720 --> 26:54.040
Yes.

26:54.300 --> 26:54.920
Segment four.

26:55.760 --> 26:56.240
Morphology.

26:56.580 --> 26:59.280
This is where we get into the generative aspect of the language.

26:59.420 --> 27:01.320
This might sound technical, but stay with us.

27:01.460 --> 27:03.480
Because it's like unlocking a secret code.

27:03.700 --> 27:06.940
This connects back to the minimal assembly concept.

27:07.260 --> 27:07.440
Yeah.

27:07.440 --> 27:10.320
How do you build a lot of meaning with very few parts?

27:10.320 --> 27:15.040
The source starts with a quick detour into numbers, which I thought was a great way to visualize this.

27:15.040 --> 27:15.560
Yes.

27:16.060 --> 27:17.960
Numerals as motor programs.

27:18.740 --> 27:19.720
Look at the number one.

27:19.820 --> 27:20.400
One stroke.

27:20.500 --> 27:21.800
Look at the Roman numeral, or three.

27:22.040 --> 27:22.660
Two strokes.

27:22.800 --> 27:23.320
Three strokes.

27:23.460 --> 27:24.280
It's tally logic.

27:24.560 --> 27:25.980
One mark for one thing.

27:26.100 --> 27:26.600
Exactly.

27:26.780 --> 27:27.300
It's additive.

27:27.300 --> 27:31.480
But try drawing nine lines every time you want to write the number nine.

27:31.620 --> 27:32.280
It's exhausting.

27:33.560 --> 27:34.380
The eye gets lost.

27:34.500 --> 27:35.360
You have to recount them.

27:35.600 --> 27:36.620
High energy cost.

27:37.040 --> 27:38.220
High error rate.

27:39.200 --> 27:42.140
So as the numbers get higher, the symbols become abstract.

27:42.920 --> 27:45.320
The shape of nine doesn't look like nine things.

27:45.420 --> 27:47.040
It's just a symbol we agreed on.

27:47.260 --> 27:50.620
Because drawing nine lines is too expensive energetically.

27:50.620 --> 27:51.100
Right.

27:51.460 --> 27:59.180
So the system uses tally logic for the low numbers, where it's cheap and switches to abstract symbolic logic for the high numbers.

27:59.560 --> 28:00.100
It's efficient.

28:00.460 --> 28:04.240
And the paper argues that the Arabic language itself works the same way.

28:04.980 --> 28:06.500
The root and pattern system.

28:06.700 --> 28:08.940
This is the crown jewel of Semitic languages.

28:09.460 --> 28:10.960
And it's very different from English.

28:11.400 --> 28:17.260
In English, if I want to say write, writer, book, and office, those words sound totally different.

28:17.260 --> 28:19.340
You just have to memorize them as separate items.

28:19.640 --> 28:19.740
Right.

28:19.840 --> 28:24.140
There's no logical sound connection between write and office.

28:24.840 --> 28:26.260
They're just random labels.

28:26.880 --> 28:29.240
In Arabic, there is a deep logical connection.

28:29.560 --> 28:31.880
You have a root, a semantic skeleton.

28:32.740 --> 28:36.620
For anything related to writing, that root is KTB.

28:37.980 --> 28:39.960
Kaf, ta, ba.

28:40.860 --> 28:43.720
Those three consonants hold the idea of writing.

28:44.100 --> 28:45.520
So KTB isn't a word.

28:45.620 --> 28:46.300
It's a concept.

28:46.300 --> 28:47.380
It's the DNA.

28:47.700 --> 28:48.720
It's the raw material.

28:49.060 --> 28:54.080
Now, to make a word, you drop that root into a pattern or a template.

28:54.480 --> 28:57.920
Think of it like an algebraic formula or a function in code.

28:58.040 --> 28:58.720
Give us an example.

28:58.900 --> 28:59.840
Walk us through the algebra.

29:00.100 --> 29:00.280
Okay.

29:00.520 --> 29:02.900
There is a pattern for someone who does the action.

29:03.300 --> 29:04.900
That pattern is fa-el.

29:05.440 --> 29:08.820
You insert KTB into that pattern and you get chi-T brighter.

29:09.140 --> 29:09.400
Okay.

29:09.680 --> 29:10.000
Chi-T.

29:10.100 --> 29:12.720
There is a pattern for place where the action happens.

29:12.880 --> 29:14.180
That pattern is mat-FL.

29:14.420 --> 29:14.560
True.

29:14.560 --> 29:18.720
You drop in KTB and you get mat-Katib office or desk.

29:18.820 --> 29:19.260
The cup.

29:19.860 --> 29:20.740
The place of writing.

29:20.900 --> 29:21.300
Exactly.

29:21.680 --> 29:22.920
There is a pattern for instrument.

29:23.160 --> 29:24.220
A pattern for passive.

29:24.780 --> 29:28.720
If I want to say it was written, the pattern is fula-kutiba.

29:28.980 --> 29:32.260
And if I want to make it reciprocal, like writing to each other.

29:32.460 --> 29:33.740
There is a pattern for that too.

29:33.880 --> 29:34.480
Form three.

29:34.740 --> 29:35.180
Kataba.

29:35.640 --> 29:36.180
To correspond.

29:36.520 --> 29:37.380
To write back and forth.

29:37.380 --> 29:38.400
It's like a code.

29:38.500 --> 29:39.640
It's incredibly logical.

29:39.640 --> 29:41.820
It is a generative engine.

29:42.160 --> 29:45.760
You don't have to memorize a dictionary of random sounds.

29:45.980 --> 29:50.300
You memorize the roots, the meanings, and the patterns, the functions.

29:50.920 --> 29:54.380
Once you have the code, you can build words you've never even heard before.

29:54.380 --> 30:02.540
If you know the root for drink, S-H-R-B, and you know the pattern for place, make F-A-L, you

30:02.540 --> 30:05.720
can guess that mash-rob means a place to drink.

30:05.880 --> 30:06.000
Ugh.

30:06.380 --> 30:08.740
And the paper connects us back to the writing script.

30:08.880 --> 30:09.180
Yes.

30:09.540 --> 30:15.660
Just as the script uses a base shape plus dot to make a letter, the language uses root plus

30:15.660 --> 30:17.140
pattern to make a word.

30:17.500 --> 30:20.500
It's the same minimal assembly philosophy.

30:20.940 --> 30:26.440
You take a stable substrate, the skeleton, and you modify it slightly to create massive

30:26.440 --> 30:27.560
amounts of new meaning.

30:27.680 --> 30:28.760
It's incredibly elegant.

30:28.900 --> 30:31.620
It's like the language and the script are running on the same operating system.

30:31.620 --> 30:36.140
They are, and there's one specific mark the author mentions that perfectly encapsulates

30:36.140 --> 30:39.660
this time-stretching generative power, the Shadda.

30:39.720 --> 30:41.040
The little W mark.

30:41.400 --> 30:44.260
Yes, the little W that sits on top of a letter.

30:44.900 --> 30:49.100
We talked about laziness earlier, but sometimes you need to emphasize something.

30:49.660 --> 30:52.680
The Shadda indicates gemination, or doubling.

30:53.420 --> 30:57.320
If you see a Shadda on a K, it's not a K, it's a K-K.

30:57.480 --> 30:58.340
You hold the sound.

30:58.520 --> 31:01.400
Like in the word bookkeeper, in English, that double K.

31:01.400 --> 31:01.880
Exactly.

31:02.540 --> 31:05.220
But in English, we usually rush through it.

31:05.720 --> 31:07.480
In Arabic, you must hold it.

31:07.580 --> 31:09.000
It creates a rhythmic stop.

31:09.480 --> 31:13.820
But here is the magic that doubling changes the meaning entirely.

31:14.240 --> 31:15.060
Give me an example.

31:15.400 --> 31:17.300
Take the root D-R-S.

31:18.200 --> 31:19.720
Dar-S means he studied.

31:20.100 --> 31:21.420
Just a simple past tense.

31:21.620 --> 31:22.180
Dar-S.

31:22.480 --> 31:23.200
He studied.

31:23.360 --> 31:25.440
Now put a Shadda on the middle letter.

31:25.820 --> 31:26.320
Dar-S.

31:26.460 --> 31:27.300
You double the R.

31:27.440 --> 31:27.820
Dar-S.

31:27.900 --> 31:28.920
That means he taught.

31:28.920 --> 31:29.680
Wait, really?

31:30.000 --> 31:32.080
He learned versus he taught.

31:32.180 --> 31:33.200
Those are opposites.

31:33.440 --> 31:35.740
And the only difference is holding the consonant longer.

31:36.060 --> 31:36.460
Yes.

31:36.960 --> 31:40.220
He studied implies he did the action to himself.

31:40.800 --> 31:42.900
He caught implies he made someone else study.

31:43.340 --> 31:47.800
That concept causative is triggered just by doubling the middle root letter.

31:47.800 --> 31:50.260
So physically, what does the Shadda represent?

31:50.860 --> 31:53.180
It represents extending the sound in time.

31:53.860 --> 31:54.780
You hold the closure.

31:55.500 --> 32:02.740
It's a tiny, mark-minimal ink that indicates a major structural change in the timing of the word.

32:03.380 --> 32:06.520
A minimal mark for a major manipulation of time.

32:06.900 --> 32:07.920
That is poetic.

32:08.960 --> 32:15.620
A tiny, squiggly W that stretches time and reverses the flow of action from learning to teaching.

32:15.620 --> 32:17.720
That's the beauty of this system.

32:17.940 --> 32:21.180
It packs so much information into such small gestures.

32:21.560 --> 32:24.360
We're heading into segment five now, and I want to zoom out a bit.

32:24.780 --> 32:30.800
We've been very technical physics, anatomy, morphology, but writing is also cultural.

32:31.000 --> 32:31.560
It's historical.

32:31.820 --> 32:33.360
It carries the ghosts of the past.

32:33.460 --> 32:33.920
It does.

32:34.000 --> 32:34.860
It carries memory.

32:35.020 --> 32:38.700
Every time you write, you are reenacting thousands of years of history.

32:39.020 --> 32:41.520
The source talks about the ancestry of the alphabet.

32:41.820 --> 32:43.040
Where does this all come from?

32:43.040 --> 32:46.240
It traces back to the Phoenician and Proto-Sinaitic scripts.

32:46.400 --> 32:47.640
This was the compression event.

32:47.960 --> 32:49.280
Before this, we had hieroglyphs.

32:49.920 --> 32:53.340
Hieroglyphs in Egypt, cuneiform in Mesopotamia.

32:53.520 --> 32:57.860
These are magnificent systems, but they were incredibly complex.

32:57.960 --> 32:59.380
You had thousands of symbols.

32:59.860 --> 33:03.120
Some stood for sounds, some for whole words, some for concepts.

33:03.700 --> 33:06.180
You had to be an elite scribe to learn them.

33:06.280 --> 33:08.300
You had to devote your life to it.

33:08.360 --> 33:10.060
It was proprietary technology.

33:10.280 --> 33:12.140
It kept the power in the hands of the few.

33:12.140 --> 33:12.860
Exactly.

33:13.640 --> 33:18.940
The invention of the alphabet around 1500 BC was a massive data compression.

33:20.100 --> 33:29.420
Some geniuses in the Sinai Peninsula, probably miners or workers, not elites, realized, wait, we don't need a picture for every word.

33:29.500 --> 33:31.740
We just need a symbol for every sound.

33:31.880 --> 33:35.400
So they compressed thousands of glyphs down to what?

33:35.620 --> 33:37.120
About 22 sounds.

33:37.340 --> 33:38.220
22 symbols.

33:38.440 --> 33:40.340
That is the ultimate minimal assembly.

33:40.340 --> 33:41.540
It changed the world.

33:41.840 --> 33:43.260
It democratized literacy.

33:43.500 --> 33:45.520
You could teach a kid the alphabet in a week.

33:45.620 --> 33:47.060
You couldn't do that with hieroglyphs.

33:47.260 --> 33:49.340
It broke the monopoly on knowledge.

33:50.060 --> 33:51.280
But here's the cool part.

33:52.060 --> 33:58.180
Even though they stripped away the pictures, the ghosts of the pictures remained in the names of the letters.

33:58.440 --> 34:01.320
This is the midrashic memory the paper discusses.

34:01.860 --> 34:02.420
Midrashic.

34:02.420 --> 34:06.320
That refers to the tradition of interpreting biblical text, right?

34:06.640 --> 34:06.940
Yes.

34:07.440 --> 34:10.880
It's finding deep symbolic meanings in the details.

34:11.860 --> 34:15.540
Take the first letter, aleph or elif in Arabic.

34:15.880 --> 34:17.620
It comes from the word for ox, right?

34:17.660 --> 34:17.980
Correct.

34:18.400 --> 34:20.140
Alp or aleph meant ox.

34:20.680 --> 34:23.280
The original drawing was an ox head with horns.

34:23.280 --> 34:28.220
Over centuries, it rotated and simplified into the letter A we use today.

34:28.740 --> 34:30.740
Turn a capital A upside down.

34:31.120 --> 34:32.120
You see the horns.

34:32.340 --> 34:32.920
I see it.

34:32.960 --> 34:34.340
It's a triangle with legs.

34:34.560 --> 34:37.700
So the form became abstract, but the meaning stuck.

34:38.080 --> 34:41.660
The ox implies strength, leadership, the first one.

34:41.820 --> 34:44.340
And the second letter, bet or bat.

34:44.540 --> 34:45.780
Bet means house.

34:45.980 --> 34:47.640
So you have the ox and the house.

34:47.880 --> 34:51.200
And the paper mentions a narrative about the ox entering the house.

34:51.200 --> 34:51.520
Right.

34:51.920 --> 34:58.040
Midrashic interpretation might say, the strong leader, ox, enters the enclosure, house.

34:58.500 --> 34:59.720
It creates a story.

35:00.340 --> 35:02.840
Now, the expert insight here is crucial.

35:03.440 --> 35:08.120
The scribes probably didn't plan this narrative when they were simplifying the shapes.

35:08.220 --> 35:09.660
They were just trying to write faster.

35:09.820 --> 35:11.880
They weren't trying to write a philosophy book in the alphabet.

35:12.040 --> 35:13.480
So it wasn't a deliberate code.

35:13.900 --> 35:14.620
Likely not.

35:14.900 --> 35:20.440
But, and this is big, the fact that people interpreted it that way helped the alphabet survive.

35:20.440 --> 35:22.240
It's cultural stabilization.

35:22.660 --> 35:23.380
Ah, I see.

35:23.880 --> 35:27.940
If you believe the letter A is just a random line, you might change it.

35:28.400 --> 35:31.180
You might decide to draw it as a circle tomorrow because you're bored.

35:31.300 --> 35:31.700
Exactly.

35:31.960 --> 35:36.580
But if you believe A is the sacred ox of leadership, you don't touch it.

35:36.720 --> 35:37.500
You preserve it.

35:37.620 --> 35:38.580
It becomes sacred.

35:39.160 --> 35:42.160
This symbolic layering acts as a cultural glue.

35:42.380 --> 35:47.080
It kept the shape stable for thousands of years, even as languages changed.

35:47.080 --> 35:48.400
That's fascinating.

35:48.540 --> 35:55.440
So the physics, laziness, kept it efficient, but the stories, symbolism, kept it protected.

35:55.700 --> 35:56.180
Exactly.

35:56.420 --> 35:58.040
A dual layer of survival.

35:58.820 --> 36:02.840
The biological pressure to simplify and the cultural pressure to preserve.

36:03.260 --> 36:06.340
There is one last historical point in this segment about ligatures.

36:06.920 --> 36:11.760
We talked about how the script flows, but in the early days, it flowed a little too wildly.

36:11.760 --> 36:14.340
It was combinatorial explosion.

36:15.280 --> 36:21.720
In early manuscripts, the shape of a letter could change drastically depending on what letter came before it and what letter came after it.

36:21.880 --> 36:26.800
So an N might look one way next to a B and totally different next to an R.

36:27.060 --> 36:27.380
Right.

36:27.860 --> 36:34.480
And if you have 28 letters and each one changes based on its neighbors, you have thousands of possible combinations.

36:35.240 --> 36:37.660
That is a huge cognitive load for a writer.

36:37.860 --> 36:40.060
You have to remember too many shapes.

36:40.060 --> 36:41.340
So how did they fix it?

36:42.100 --> 36:42.660
Standardization.

36:43.280 --> 36:45.580
They boiled it down to just four positions.

36:46.420 --> 36:53.140
Initial, start of word, medial, middle, final, end, and isolated, alone.

36:53.380 --> 36:53.840
That's it.

36:54.080 --> 36:54.660
Just four.

36:54.900 --> 36:55.420
Just four.

36:55.780 --> 36:59.780
Instead of infinite variations, you only need to learn four shapes per letter.

37:00.380 --> 37:04.320
And honestly, for most letters, the initial and medial are basically the same.

37:04.800 --> 37:06.960
This was another massive optimization.

37:07.440 --> 37:09.600
It reduced the search space for the brain.

37:09.600 --> 37:14.120
It's like they were constantly patching the software to make it run smoother on the human hardware.

37:14.480 --> 37:15.640
That is the perfect metaphor.

37:15.860 --> 37:17.760
The hardware is our hand and eye.

37:18.060 --> 37:19.340
The software is the script.

37:19.760 --> 37:25.680
And for centuries, they were optimizing the code to run as efficiently as possible on that biological machine.

37:26.120 --> 37:32.700
So bringing this all together, we started with the mission to understand how laziness and clarity fought each other.

37:32.920 --> 37:37.040
And we found that the Arabic script is essentially a peace treaty between them.

37:37.360 --> 37:38.220
It is an equilibrium.

37:38.220 --> 37:40.160
Let's recap the balance points.

37:40.480 --> 37:45.880
It balances speed, cursive, against legibility, dots, and non-connecting letters.

37:46.100 --> 37:52.520
It balances the skeleton, the motor flow, against the augmentation, the specific information.

37:53.000 --> 37:57.760
It balances the root, the concept, against the pattern, the function.

37:57.760 --> 38:03.240
It really is a durable residue of embodied production, to quote the paper again.

38:03.720 --> 38:07.780
It looks the way it does because human hands are lazy and human eyes are picky.

38:07.960 --> 38:08.940
And because it worked.

38:09.320 --> 38:10.200
It survived.

38:10.580 --> 38:14.020
It transmitted knowledge across empires and centuries.

38:14.340 --> 38:17.080
It is an incredibly successful piece of technology.

38:17.080 --> 38:22.600
Now, before we go, the expert in the source poses a final provocative thought.

38:22.960 --> 38:26.200
And this is something I want our listeners to really mull over.

38:26.460 --> 38:26.880
Yes.

38:27.240 --> 38:30.200
We've spent this whole hour talking about embodied constraint.

38:30.540 --> 38:34.280
The ink flow, the friction of the pen, the cramp of the hand, but...

38:34.280 --> 38:35.440
We don't write like that anymore.

38:35.580 --> 38:36.080
We type.

38:36.260 --> 38:38.900
I am tapping on glass or clicking plastic keys.

38:38.900 --> 38:40.260
The friction is gone.

38:40.620 --> 38:41.620
The ink is gone.

38:42.020 --> 38:44.260
The motor constraint of the curve is gone.

38:44.400 --> 38:45.060
Think about it.

38:45.340 --> 38:49.540
The energy cost of typing a Z is the exact same as typing an A.

38:49.960 --> 38:50.800
It's just a keystroke.

38:51.300 --> 38:56.360
The energy cost of typing a complex letter like shin is the same as a simple letter like ba.

38:56.540 --> 38:57.080
Whoa.

38:57.480 --> 39:02.360
So the economic pressure that shaped the script has evaporated.

39:02.480 --> 39:03.660
It has shifted entirely.

39:03.900 --> 39:05.980
The thermodynamic landscape has changed.

39:05.980 --> 39:12.340
So the question is, if writing systems evolve like biological organisms under pressure,

39:12.680 --> 39:16.140
what happens now that the pressure is gone or changed?

39:16.480 --> 39:18.680
Will our scripts lose their efficiency?

39:19.100 --> 39:20.140
Will they become bloated?

39:20.360 --> 39:23.180
Or will we see new forms of dots and ligatures?

39:23.420 --> 39:24.420
Think about emojis.

39:24.820 --> 39:25.340
Emojis.

39:25.460 --> 39:26.520
Think about a text message.

39:26.820 --> 39:28.380
It's often ambiguous, right?

39:28.720 --> 39:30.320
Tone is hard to read in plain text.

39:30.700 --> 39:31.060
Fine.

39:31.140 --> 39:33.000
Does that mean I'm good or I'm angry?

39:33.260 --> 39:35.460
It's the RASM problem all over again.

39:35.460 --> 39:36.660
It's ambiguous.

39:37.120 --> 39:39.500
The letters are clear, but the intent is blurry.

39:39.900 --> 39:40.600
So what do we do?

39:40.880 --> 39:42.360
We add a smiley face.

39:43.100 --> 39:44.460
Or a thumbs up.

39:44.560 --> 39:45.620
Or a spell emoji.

39:45.840 --> 39:46.620
We add a dot.

39:46.880 --> 39:47.640
We add a dot.

39:48.380 --> 39:50.480
The emoji is the new diacritic.

39:51.180 --> 39:55.640
It is a minimal assembly added to the text to clarify the emotional intent.

39:56.160 --> 40:01.640
It's cheap energy-wise, just a click, but it has high informational return.

40:01.920 --> 40:03.080
That is a wild thought.

40:03.380 --> 40:05.040
Emojis are the new vowel marks.

40:05.040 --> 40:08.480
We might be watching a new stabilization happen in real time.

40:09.160 --> 40:13.860
The digital age is a new environment, and the organism of language is adapting again.

40:14.400 --> 40:18.460
It's finding a new equilibrium between speed and clarity.

40:18.880 --> 40:21.460
I'm going to be thinking about that every time I'm in the text message now.

40:22.240 --> 40:25.940
Every time I hesitate and add a lull, just to make sure I don't sound mean.

40:25.940 --> 40:29.960
That lull is you being an efficient scribe, preventing ambiguity.

40:30.480 --> 40:31.920
You are part of the evolution.

40:32.560 --> 40:35.460
Well, on that note, we are going to wrap up this deep dive.

40:35.940 --> 40:40.940
I hope you look at the next piece of writing you see, whether it's Arabic, English, or a text message,

40:41.100 --> 40:44.720
and see the invisible history of muscles and nerves behind it.

40:44.780 --> 40:47.360
It's all just human energy, preserved in ink and pixels.

40:47.360 --> 40:48.440
Thanks for listening.

40:48.720 --> 40:50.060
We'll see you in the next deep dive.

